    {
      "name": "No traumatic amputation documented BEFORE the index admission",
      "description": "Zero traumatic-amputation records in [index - 30d, index - 1d] -- STRICTLY BEFORE the admission, changed 2026-08-12 from a +/-30d window that excluded 220 of 935 real Duke patients (24%). That figure is not clinically credible for a cohort already filtered to PAD/diabetes/wound: all 220 had passed the dysvascular indication rule. The likely mechanism is CODING OVERLAP -- this concept's descendants are 'Traumatic amputation above knee', '... below knee', '... of foot', which is exactly how a coder may describe the surgical amputation that just happened, not the mechanism that caused it. A +/-30d window therefore catches the index amputation's own coding. A genuine traumatic amputation is preceded by the injury; a miscode of the surgery appears on or after the admission date. Restricting to strictly-prior separates them as far as OMOP allows -- circe has no 'NOT at this visit' operator, so the index encounter cannot be excluded directly. THIS MAKES THE NEXT RUN A TEST: if the exclusion collapses from 220 to a small number, the codes were on the index admission and the miscoding hypothesis is confirmed; if it stays near 220, the trauma genuinely precedes the admission and these are real traumatic cases. ACCEPTED COST: a same-day trauma-to-amputation case (injury and amputation coded on the admission date) is no longer excluded.",
